    "title": "Day of prayer in Slidell seeks to heal political divide - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Rick Gogreve ,\n1 hours ago SLIDELL, La. ( WGNO ) — Following a string of violent political attacks nationally, two Slidell councilmembers want to change the narrative.\nInstead of becoming fearful in a time of divisiveness, they’re hopeful for a peaceful future.\nThey decided that now is the time to call for peace and declared Wednesday a day of prayer.\n“We had a senator in Minnesota that was shot, we had a city councilman in Virginia where somebody just walked in, threw gas on him and lit him on fire; we had the assassination of Charlie Kirk. This is happening to people on both sides of the aisle, but the violence honestly has to stop,” Slidell City Council Member Trey Brownfield said.\nTrial delayed for teens accused in death of St. Tammany Parish sergeant While Denham is a Democrat, Brownfield is a Republican. They say that their commitment to peace in spite of their differences will help to spread their message.\n“Our beliefs are very different but at the end of the day they’re the same. We want what’s best for our community. We want what’s best for our nation,” City Council Member Leslie Denham said.\n“We sit on the opposite sides of the aisle politically, but we’re great friends at the end of the day and our friendship and what we have, we want to shine that to other people,” Brownfield said.\nMany residents at the prayer service say that this message of love and togetherness is key in today’s world.\n“I have to respect somebody else’s opinion that’s different than me. It doesn’t mean that I know everything or that my way is the only way. I have to be open to listening to other people’s opinion,” Slidell resident Lori Anselmo said.\n“You got to get your folks together and it’s got to be live. It needs to be person to person so they have a true understanding of what you are saying and what you are feeling,” Slidell resident Rene Arsemont said.\nStay up to date with the latest news, weather and sports by downloading the WGNO app on the Apple or Google Play stores and by subscribing to the WGNO newsletter .\nLatest Posts Pleasant weekend behind Thursday front Day of prayer in Slidell seeks to heal political divide White House directs agencies to prep for mass firings ahead of potential shutdown Watch: SEC Network’s Gene Chizik previews LSU’s week five matchup with Ole Miss Siblings face smuggling charges after agent sees ‘pair of feet sticking out’ in back of SUV Copyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to WGNO.",
